TOPICS = [
    {"id": "T1", "title": "Electrochemistry and the two types of cell",
     "overview": "Electrochemistry deals with the interconversion of chemical energy and electrical energy. A galvanic cell "
                 "converts the chemical energy of a spontaneous redox reaction into electrical energy (batteries, fuel cells); "
                 "an electrolytic cell uses electrical energy to drive a non-spontaneous redox reaction (electroplating, "
                 "electrorefining). Every electrochemical system needs three things: a redox reaction, electrodes (anode for "
                 "oxidation, cathode for reduction) and an electrolyte that conducts by the movement of ions."},
    {"id": "T2", "title": "Electrode potential and its origin: the Helmholtz double layer",
     "overview": "When a metal rod is dipped in a solution of its own ions, a potential develops at the metal–solution interface: "
                 "the electrode potential $E$. It arises because two opposite reactions compete, ionisation "
                 "($\\mathrm{M\\rightarrow M^{n+}+ne^-}$) and deposition ($\\mathrm{M^{n+}+ne^-\\rightarrow M}$). Whichever is faster "
                 "charges the metal (negative in case I, positive in case II); the charged metal attracts a layer of oppositely "
                 "charged ions, forming the Helmholtz electrical double layer, and an equilibrium is set up."},
    {"id": "T3", "title": "Standard electrode potential, electrochemical cell and cell notation",
     "overview": "Electrode potential depends on the metal, the ion concentration, the temperature and (for gas electrodes) the "
                 "pressure, so electrodes are compared through the standard electrode potential $E^\\circ$: unit concentration, "
                 "298 K and 1 atm. A single electrode potential cannot be measured, so two electrodes are coupled into an "
                 "electrochemical cell. The cell is written in a standard notation, anode first: "
                 "$\\mathrm{Zn(s)\\,|\\,Zn^{2+}(1\\,M)\\,||\\,Cu^{2+}(1\\,M)\\,|\\,Cu(s)}$ for the Daniell cell."},
    {"id": "T4", "title": "Cell potential, standard cell potential and free energy",
     "overview": "The cell potential $E_{cell}$ is the difference between the electrode potentials of the two electrodes; under "
                 "standard conditions (1 M, 1 atm, 298 K) it is the standard cell potential $E^\\circ_{cell}$. It is calculated as "
                 "$E_{cell}=E_{rhs}-E_{lhs}=E_{cathode}-E_{anode}$, using reduction potentials for both. $E_{cell}$ is the "
                 "driving force of the cell reaction: $\\Delta G=-nFE_{cell}$, so a spontaneous reaction ($\\Delta G<0$) has a "
                 "positive $E_{cell}$. This convention is used in every cell numerical in the ESA papers."},
    {"id": "T5", "title": "The electrochemical series and its uses",
     "overview": "Arranging electrodes in order of their standard reduction potentials gives the electrochemical series, from "
                 "lithium ($-3.03$ V) to gold ($+1.50$ V on the slide). A negative value indicates an oxidation tendency and a "
                 "positive value a reduction tendency. The series predicts which electrode is the anode, whether a reaction is "
                 "spontaneous, and which metal corrodes when two metals are in contact (galvanised versus tinned iron)."},
]

