STATEMENTS = [
    {"topic": "T2", "text": "When a metal M is in contact with solution containing its ions $\\mathrm{M^{n+}}$, two reactions are possible: 1. Ionisation (oxidation) $\\mathrm{M\\rightleftharpoons M^{n+}+ne^-}$; 2. Deposition (reduction) $\\mathrm{M^{n+}+ne^-\\rightleftharpoons M}$.", "srcs": ["S001·5"]},
    {"topic": "T2", "text": "Case I: if ionization is faster than deposition, the metal acquires net negative charge, consequently retards the rate of ionization and increases the rate of deposition. This ultimately leads to the establishment of equilibrium. The metal electrode gets negatively charged and attracts the layer of positive ions at the interface; an electrical double layer is formed at the interface of metal and solution known as Helmholtz electrical double layer.", "srcs": ["S001·6"]},
    {"topic": "T2", "text": "Case II: if deposition is faster than ionization, the metal acquires net positive charge, consequently retards the rate of deposition and increases the rate of ionization. This ultimately leads to the establishment of equilibrium. The metal electrode gets positively charged and attracts the layer of negative ions at the interface; an electrical double layer is formed, known as Helmholtz electrical double layer.", "srcs": ["S001·7"]},
    {"topic": "T4", "text": "$E_{cell}=E_{rhs}-E_{lhs}=E_{cathode}-E_{anode}$. $E_{cell}$ represents the driving force for the cell reaction to take place: $\\Delta G=-nFE_{cell}$. If the reaction is spontaneous $\\Delta G$ is negative, thus $E_{cell}$ should be positive; if the reaction is non-spontaneous $\\Delta G$ is positive, thus $E_{cell}$ should be negative.", "srcs": ["S001·9"]},
    {"topic": "T5", "text": "A negative value indicates oxidation tendency while a positive value indicates a reduction tendency.", "srcs": ["S001·10"]},
]

