NOTES = [
    {"topic": "T1", "text": "Electrochemistry: interconversion of chemical and electrical energy. Galvanic cell: spontaneous redox reaction → electrical energy (batteries, fuel cells). Electrolytic cell: external energy drives a non-spontaneous reaction (electroplating). Needs: a redox reaction; anode (oxidation) and cathode (reduction); an ionic electrolyte (solution, melt or solid)."},
    {"topic": "T2", "text": "Electrode potential $E$: the potential at the interface of a metal and a solution of its own ions. Origin: ionisation $\\mathrm{M\\rightleftharpoons M^{n+}+ne^-}$ and deposition $\\mathrm{M^{n+}+ne^-\\rightleftharpoons M}$ compete. Case I (ionisation faster): metal negative, attracts cations. Case II (deposition faster): metal positive, attracts anions. The two charge layers form the Helmholtz electrical double layer."},
    {"topic": "T3", "text": "$E^\\circ$: electrode potential at unit concentration, 298 K and 1 atm for gases; depends only on the electrode. Two electrodes form a cell (single potentials are not measurable). Notation: anode | anode solution || cathode solution | cathode, e.g. $\\mathrm{Zn|Zn^{2+}||Cu^{2+}|Cu}$."},
    {"topic": "T4", "text": "$E_{cell}$ = difference of the electrode potentials = $E_{rhs}-E_{lhs}=E_{cathode}-E_{anode}$; standard (1 M, 1 atm, 298 K): $E^\\circ_{cell}$. $\\Delta G=-nFE_{cell}$: spontaneous ⇔ $\\Delta G<0$ ⇔ $E_{cell}>0$. Daniell: $E^\\circ_{cell}=1.10$ V, $\\Delta G^\\circ=-212.3$ kJ mol$^{-1}$."},
    {"topic": "T5", "text": "Electrochemical series: electrodes in order of standard reduction potential, Li $-3.03$ … $\\mathrm{H_2}$ 0.00 … Au $+1.50$ V (slide). Negative = oxidation tendency (strong reducing metals); positive = reduction tendency. Lower $E^\\circ$ = anode. Uses: $E^\\circ_{cell}$, spontaneity, displacement, corrosion (Zn protects Fe; Sn only while intact)."},
]

